# Feedback

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

Feedback invites people to say what was unclear or broken, tells them where it goes, and confirms honestly what happened. It comes after the main task and never gets in the way of a device control.

This page owns the delivery and privacy rules for feedback. The [publishing variant](../publishing/components/feedback.html) keeps its contextual form and its public issue examples. An urgent household message is a different job: see [Show household urgency clearly](../patterns/household-urgency.html).

This page covers:

- Feedback
  - Civica feedback pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

- An invitation: one question in plain words.
- A route to a real channel: a link to a public issue form, or a form that reaches a receiving service.
- Guidance on what to leave out, such as passwords, access tokens and private sensor history.
- An outcome message that says what is known.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-feedback-pattern">Civica feedback pattern</h3>

A Civica pattern with no Home Assistant element behind it. Home Assistant has no feedback service and the Civica theme does not create one. The documentation footer links to the project’s public issue form; that link is ordinary navigation.

The Civica theme sets no variable for this pattern. A feedback form takes the theme’s own field and text colours: hint text uses the secondary text colour (#484949 in light mode, #CBD0D3 in dark mode), and an error uses the error colour (#CA3535 in light mode, #F0C6C6 in dark mode).

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A link to a public issue form needs no receiving service of your own, but what people write becomes public. Say so before they click.
- A form needs a service that receives it. Do not show a form that sends nowhere.
- Do not imply that someone reads feedback all the time, or that a reply will come, unless that is true.
- A local preview must say that nothing was sent.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Before offering a submission, say where it goes, whether it is public and whether a reply is expected. Ask only for what the receiving service needs, and show an example of useful detail.

- Confirm receipt only when the receiving service has confirmed it. A request that has been sent but not answered is still pending.
- If sending fails, keep what the person wrote and do not send it again automatically. If the outcome is uncertain, say so before inviting a retry that could duplicate the report.
- Do not attach household settings, sensor values or screenshots automatically. Explain how to remove private information before posting in public.
- Do not require a positive rating, and never delay a safety action for feedback.
- Keep a separate route for help with completing a task.

Success criteria that apply:

- 3.3.1 Error Identification, 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions and 3.3.3 Error Suggestion: a visible label, and an error that says what to change.
- 3.3.7 Redundant Entry: keep what someone typed if sending fails.
- 3.3.8 Accessible Authentication (Minimum): do not ask people to solve a puzzle to send feedback.
- 4.1.3 Status Messages: the outcome is announced without moving focus.
- 2.4.4 Link Purpose (In Context): the link says what it opens.

One question, a clear destination and a plain outcome keep the load low.

<h3 id="check">Check it works</h3>

Test an empty comment, a very long comment, a failed send and a repeated press of the button. See [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html).

